  ☐ Implement industry-standard dynamic camera contrast adjustment for the HDR scene.
    - Implementation contract and remaining visual/performance acceptance: docs/PBR.CameraExposure.md.
      GPU histogram metering and bounded temporal exposure precede the existing stable tone curve;
      lighting/refraction stay unexposed. Persisted automatic/manual controls share one camera model.
    - Investigate established automatic exposure and adaptive tone-mapping approaches; define the
      intended camera response, metering units and contrast behavior before selecting an algorithm.
      Distinguish exposure adaptation from changes to the tone curve; do not substitute independent
      per-frame minimum/maximum normalization for a temporally stable camera model.
    - Meter scene-linear luminance after scene/OIT/late composition and before display conversion.
      Evaluate bounded GPU histogram/percentile or log-luminance reduction with robust rejection of
      isolated solar/emissive peaks and near-black outliers. Exclude UI/offscreen draws and define
      metering relative to bloom/light shafts so generated glare cannot drive an exposure feedback loop.
    - Define exposure compensation, a middle-gray target, minimum/maximum exposure in stops and
      configurable metering behavior. Adapt over elapsed time with independently bounded brightening
      and darkening rates; prevent pumping, flicker and frame-rate-dependent transitions.
    - Define highlight shoulder, shadow toe and midtone contrast while preserving color ratios,
      highlight detail and dark-scene intent. Evaluate whether exposure plus a stable tone curve
      satisfies the requirement before adding local contrast adaptation or its additional history.
    - Apply the camera response once at the shared final display boundary. Keep lighting caches,
      atmospheric radiance and reflection/refraction inputs unexposed; coordinate any exposure-relative
      bloom thresholds with the owned postprocessing tasks. Preserve grading order and final dithering.
    - Integrate through the existing VGE-owned postprocessing and final composition owners;
      scheduling, intermediate targets and final-shader ownership are documented in docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md.
    - Use existing VGE shader, binding, PSO and resource owners with the GLSL 330 baseline; bound
      reduction cost and history storage and avoid synchronous per-frame CPU luminance readback.
      Define startup, camera-cut, teleport, world change, resize/reload and settings-reset behavior.
    - Expose persisted controls through the existing graphics configuration, including a disable/manual
      mode that retains predictable fixed exposure. Support both LumOn modes and current SDR output;
      keep future native-HDR presentation compatible without making it an implementation prerequisite.
    - Validate bright exterior/dark interior transitions, sun entering/leaving the frame, emissive
      objects, underwater views, day/night changes and stationary scenes at varied frame rates.
      Use focused subagent numerical/GPU tests for metering, adaptation, bounds, lifecycle and single
      display conversion; obtain user-run visual acceptance and measure GPU time, bandwidth and memory.

